<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    accident_slowzone.add.xml
    ===========================
    A variableSpeedSign (VSS) - real, documented SUMO additional-file
    element (https://sumo.dlr.de/docs/Simulation/Variable_Speed_Signs.html)
    - covering all three East approach lanes so the accident produces
    genuine congestion across the whole approach, not just in the one
    lane the stalled truck physically occupies (see accident.rou.xml).

    Honest limitation, stated plainly: a variableSpeedSign applies to
    the entire lane it's placed on for as long as it's active - SUMO
    has no mechanism for a slow zone localized to a small radius around
    a specific point within a lane. This is confirmed against SUMO's
    own documentation and issue tracker, not assumed. Since E_in is a
    single short approach block, applying the crawl to the whole lane
    is a reasonable visual proxy for "slow near the accident," but it
    is genuinely the whole lane, every vehicle on it, for the active
    window - not a small zone around the truck specifically.

    Three steps:
      t=100  speed drops to 2.78 m/s (~10 km/h) on all three East
             lanes - matches the truck's stop start, forcing a
             visible crawl through the whole approach while it's
             blocked.
      t=650  speed rises to 8.33 m/s (~30 km/h) - the truck's stop
             ends here too (see accident.rou.xml), so this is the
             start of recovery, not instant normal-speed traffic.
      t=680  speed="-1" resets the lane to its own real speed limit
             (13.89 m/s / 50 km/h, per intersection.type.xml) rather
             than hard-coding that number here, so this file can never
             silently drift out of sync with the network's actual
             speed limit if that ever changes.
-->
<additional>
    <variableSpeedSign id="accident_slowzone" lanes="E_in_0 E_in_1 E_in_2">
        <step time="100" speed="2.78"/>
        <step time="650" speed="8.33"/>
        <step time="680" speed="-1"/>
    </variableSpeedSign>
</additional>